  "response": "## Solution\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem with:\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|¬D) = 0.95, so P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**P(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)**\n= 0.98 × 0.002 + 0.05 × 0.998\n= 0.00196 + 0.0499\n= 0.05186\n\n**P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+)**\n= 0.00196 / 0.05186\n= 0.037794...\n≈ **3.78%**\n\nDespite testing positive, this person is more likely *not* to have the condition (only ~3.78% chance they do) — a classic illustration of how low base rates dominate outcomes even with a fairly accurate test.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
